.TH FATFS 4
.SH NAME
fatfs \- FAT file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fatfs
.I device
[
.I srvname
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fatfs
serves a FAT12, FAT16 or FAT32 volume as a 9P tree.
The kind is decided by the volume's cluster count, as the specification
says, and not by the label the formatter wrote.
.I Device
is opened and read and written at an offset (see
.IR read (2))
and may be a partition, a whole disk, or a file in another filesystem;
.I fatfs
cannot tell which.
It posts
.I srvname
.RB ( /srv/esp
when not given), then forks: the parent exits once the name is there, so a
shell that started it can mount on the next line, and the child serves
until the last mount and the name are both gone.
.PP
The boot mounts the machine's own boot volume this way, at
.B /n/esp
(see
.IR intro (1)).
.PP
Every file and directory has a long name, read from the entries FAT keeps
before the 8.3 one, or the 8.3 name itself when a file has no other.
A file is created with whatever name it is given: a name that is already a
valid uppercase 8.3 name keeps it, and any other gets a short name built
the way Windows builds one -- squeezed, uppercased, cut to six characters,
and given
.B ~1
or the next number free -- alongside the long name that spells it exactly.
Matching is case-insensitive for ASCII, so
.B Echo
and
.B echo
name the same file.
.PP
Every change reaches the disk as it is made: a directory entry when a size
changes, a table entry when a cluster is claimed, the data as it is
written.
There is no cache the disk does not also hold, because the host is
watching this volume through QEMU and a change that sits in memory only is
a change the host may never see.
.PP
FAT has no owner, no group, and one permission bit: the read-only
attribute.
.I Fatfs
reports every file as readable and writable by everyone, less the write
bits when the attribute is set, and a change of mode (see
.IR getattr (5))
sets or clears the attribute from whether any write bit was asked for.
A directory is always searchable.
.PP
The server stops on the pipe's hang-up, when the last mount and the posted
name are both gone.
.SH FILES
.TP
.BI /srv/ srvname
the posted service,
.B esp
by default
.SH SOURCE
.B servers/fatfs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bind (1),
.IR read (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR getattr (5),
.IR sd (3)
.SH BUGS
There is no rename.
.I Cp
and
.I mv
(see
.IR cp (1))
copy and remove instead.
A file is limited to
4294967295
bytes, FAT32's own ceiling.
